{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The lighthouse keeper's daughter kept a ledger of ships that never arrived. She had started it at eleven, after a schooner called the Perpetua was reported two days out of Halifax and then simply was not, and she had filled three notebooks since. Each entry had the same columns: name, date last seen, cargo if known, weather. She did not write anything in the column she had labeled Reason, though she left it there, a thin blank strip down the right side of every page, in case one day a reason came.

Her father thought the habit was morbid. Her mother thought it was a kind of prayer. Ines herself did not think about it much; she only knew that when the fog rolled in thick enough to swallow the lamp's beam, she felt steadier with the ledger open on her knees, pen uncapped, waiting.

The night the Corvina went missing, she was nineteen. The wireless had crackled for an hour and then stopped. Ines wrote the name carefully, the date, the cargo of salted cod and one passenger. Under weather she wrote: clear. Then she sat for a long time looking at the empty Reason column, and for the first time in eight years she wrote something in it. Not an explanation. Just a question mark, small and neat, the way you might leave a door ajar for someone who has not said whether they are coming home."}
